UPDATE public.stories SET title = 'This can change your life. Empirical evidence that we all learn at the same rate. You just have to put in the effort.  Source: Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences (PNAS) - Psychological and Cognitive Sciences - an astonishing regularity in student learning rate #science #facts #stem #psychology #cognitivescience #todayilearned #themoreyouknow #interesting #interestingfacts #researchpaper  #eli5 #learnontiktok #edutok   Are there topics you believe it’s impossible for you to learn like quantum mechanics or advanced mathematics, because you feel you’re not smart?      Hey how you doing, I’m a stem creator that likes to read research papers sometimes and try to explain it to you in a language that you would understand      This study shows that everybody learns at almost the same rate, there is no such thing as fast or slow learners, here’s how they prove that      We know obviously there are people that appear to learn faster, some get As and some get Cs, and it looks into why that is as well.      This is how the study was done, a bunch of students around 7,000 to be more precise, from elementary to college level, gave exams on different topics like Math, Science and Language      Of course they gave exams that matched their level of education      Students in the lower half got a score of 55% on average, and stronger students in the upper half got 75%      Now this is already kind of odd, as the paper explains as well, this is highly variable, these are students that had met the prerequisites to take that exam, they should be almost at the same level, but they’re not.      Anyways after taking the initial exam, they were given an opportunity to sit down and study, and then they took another exam      In fact they would keep repeating this process, study exam, study exam, until they reached a score of 80%      This chart in the study shows 3 random students and how much they improved after each opportunity of sitting down and studying      The slope of these lines would be the learning rate of each student, and we can see they are very much so parallel      And if we add a bunch more students at different levels and on different topics we would get these charts in the study,       again the learning rates which is the slope of the lines is just the same      As I said, a student in the bottom 25 percentile, got a score of 55% on the first exam, these students needed about 13 opportunities to sit down and study to get to a score of 80% on the exam.      On the other hand, a student in the top 75 percentile, got a score of 75% on the first exam and needed just about 4 opportunities to sit down and study to get to a score of 80%.      But this doesn’t show us learning rate, it just shows us the importance of initial knowledge, so your takeaway from this should be that one reason some students get As and some get Cs is because they just have more initial knowledge      Learning rate would be how much they improved after each time of sitting down and studying, the stronger students would improve 2.5% after each session and the weaker students would improve 1.7%      That’s not a big difference at all, that’s less than 1%, which again shows everybody learns at almost the same rate      So apart from the initial knowledge, what really matters is the opportunity, how many times you sit down and actually study something.      This was true for all the topics and here is a paragraph from the study that truly moved me which I’m gonna use to close my video      Can anyone learn to be good at anything they want? Or is talent, like having a “knack for math” or a “gift for language,” required? Our evidence suggests that given favorable learning conditions for deliberate practice and given the learner invests effort in sufficient learning opportunities, indeed, anyone can learn anything they want.      So there really isn’t anything holding you back from learning quantum mechanics or advanced mathematics or in fact anything, the only thing holding you back is you.#CapCut ' WHERE slug = 'tiktok-7250182305203899690';
